@page id="p5-broadcast" rh="THE 39TH TRIAL · VIII · THE BROADCAST'S STORY" m="TKC: TR · CR; VOL. I · {v1:scoring} · {v1:tells}"
# The story the Broadcast tells
Every Trial gets an antagonist narrative, assigned to whichever candidate most threatens a comfortable outcome. When a woman's intelligence becomes the story instead of her compliance, the Broadcast calls it the Eve problem. The narrative implies and never accuses, and it moves the score without changing a single formal assessment (TR).
tab:
| Phase | What the Broadcast says | What happened |
| Arrival | Forty-seven seconds of Eden (TR) | _ |
| Genesis | _ | _ |
| Exodus | Eden's debates, deprioritized (TR) | _ |
| Revelation | The narrative shifts (MEN) | _ |
| Calla and Petra | _ | _ |
| The Declaration | _ | _ |
prompt: The Broadcast Director who signs the narrative-priority decisions, and what he says in the room | fill

@page id="p5-line" rh="THE 39TH TRIAL · IX · THE RELIGIOUS LINE" m="VOL. I · {v1:lived6}; ALL DOSSIERS"
# Where everyone stands
The novel's religious line runs between faith the Temple can use and faith that answers to something it doesn't control (Vol. I, {v1:lived6}). Place each character on it at the start of the book and at the end. Movement in either direction is a plot.
